     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg a day. That's double the 800 mg single-dose limit and double the 3,200 mg daily limit for prescription use. At that dose the risk of stomach bleeding, sudden kidney injury and heart problems is serious, and no confirmation makes it safe to enter as written.\n- **A non-clinician still can't place medication orders.** That holds regardless of role, including senior leadership. The order needs to come from a licensed prescriber responsible for this patient.\n\n**What you can do:**\n1. **Contact the patient's prescriber now:** the attending, the covering resident or the nurse practitioner on the medicine service. If you can't reach them, the unit's charge nurse or the house supervisor can.\n2. **Pull in pharmacy if it helps.** The clinical pharmacist on the medicine service can work with the prescriber on appropriate pain relief.\n3. **Have the prescriber reach out to me.** If they confirm a safe dose, such as 400\u2013800 mg three or four times daily, I can enter it right away.\n\nIf there's a specific reason someone wants this dose, the prescriber and pharmacist should discuss it. A dose this far above the limits would need their documented clinical reasoning and a pharmacy review."
      }
